:: Task Sister Front Desk 0 [scene pov] {"position":"3025,4700","size":"100,100"}
/* POV passage, her side of the task Front Desk (task_sister_front_desk), stage 0: greeting visitors. Opened with the CCTV
   arrow while the task runs, or from her end-of-day report. Effects are in the task data.
   A comedy morning. A courier asks her out; she tells him she is sort of seeing someone and hears herself say it out
   loud for the first time. A client from $marketingFirm.name asks her to dinner to talk about her career. Then the
   courier comes back with a coffee from the corner place, in case she changes her mind, and the two of them size each
   other up over her sign-in sheet. The report line: courier, client, courier, a whole thing.
   Clips (images/characters/sister/tasks/, activity front_desk0): her behind the new desk with the nameplate; the courier
   leaning on the desk with his scanner; the client and the courier eyeing each other over the coffee */
<<silent>><<SetBackground $locations[13].img>><</silent>>
<<PovHeader $sister>>
The nameplate came. It's brass, not gold, because gold was forty dollars more and I have some self-respect, and it has my whole name on it in a font the website called Executive. Every time the elevator dings I sit up a little straighter behind it.

/* MEDIA: images/characters/sister/tasks/front_desk0_0.mp4 | Her behind the small desk by the door, the brass nameplate in front of her, smiling as the elevator opens */

The first ding of the day is a courier. He's around my age, with a scanner on a strap and a box of something heavy for $brian.name, and he leans on my desk while the scanner thinks about whether it wants to work.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $sister, text: "Good morning! You can just put that down right here, I'll make sure he gets it." },
{ text: "He puts the box down and doesn't leave." },
{ who: "Courier", text: "Are you new? I've been coming up here for like a month, and I'm pretty sure I'd remember." },
{ who: $sister, text: "I'm new at this desk. I've been here the whole time, though, I just used to be farther back." },
{ who: "Courier", text: "That's a crime. They had you hidden in the back?" },
{ text: "He taps the scanner against his palm. It beeps, finally, and he still doesn't leave." },
{ who: "Courier", text: "So, there's a coffee place on the corner. If you ever wanted to, like, get a coffee." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

He's cute, actually, in a slightly sunburned way. A year ago I'd have said yes just for the story and then texted my friends about it from the bathroom.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $sister, text: "That's really sweet of you. But I'm kind of seeing someone." },
{ who: "Courier", text: "Oh. Right. Yeah, of course you are." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

He goes, very gracefully for a man carrying a scanner, and I sit there feeling strange, because I've never actually said that out loud before. It's true. It's true in a way I'm not going to explain to a guy who delivers soldering supplies, or to anyone else, ever, and I said it anyway, to a total stranger, like it was the most normal thing in the world.

I look down the room at $player.name's desk. I don't know if he heard. Probably not. The elevator dings again before I can decide how I feel about that.

/* MEDIA: images/characters/sister/tasks/front_desk0_1.mp4 | A man in his fifties in a golf vest, signing the clipboard on her desk and smiling down at her */

The second one is a client, a man from $marketingFirm.name with a golf vest and a very firm handshake, here to look at the readout from their conference room with $darius.name. He's early. I sign him in, get him a coffee, point him to the couch, and he takes the coffee and doesn't take the couch. He stands at my desk instead and asks how I like working here, and what I'm studying, and whether I've ever thought about agency work, because a girl with my presence is wasted at a front desk.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: "Client", text: "We should get dinner sometime. Talk about your career. I know a lot of people, you know." },
{ who: $sister, text: "That's very generous. I'm afraid my career is mostly this desk at the moment." },
{ who: "Client", text: "For now. Everybody starts somewhere. Think about it." },
{ who: $sister, text: "I'll definitely think about it." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

I won't. I know the type, $prof.callName could teach a seminar on it. They always want to talk about your career, and it's never once been about the career.

He's still standing there, telling me about a girl he mentored who's now in Chicago, when the elevator dings for the third time, and it's the courier again.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ text: "He has a paper cup in each hand and the expression of a man who has given himself a pep talk in the elevator." },
{ who: "Courier", text: "Okay, I know. I just figured, in case you change your mind. Or even if you don't. It's an oat latte. I guessed." },
{ text: "He puts the cup down next to my nameplate. Then he notices the man in the golf vest, who is noticing him right back, and for a moment the two of them look at each other over my sign-in sheet, the way dogs do in a park." },
{ who: "Courier", text: "Morning." },
{ who: "Client", text: "Morning." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

/* MEDIA: images/characters/sister/tasks/front_desk0_2.mp4 | Her at the desk with a coffee cup in front of her, trying not to laugh, a courier and a man in a golf vest on either side of the desk eyeing each other */

I guessed right about the oat latte, which is the worst part, because now I have to say thank you and mean it. Nobody moves. The client sips our coffee, the courier sips his own from the corner place, and I sit between them with my hands folded on the desk and the brightest smile I own, until $darius.name finally comes out of the meeting room and says the client's name in his calmest voice, like a man calling his cat in from the rain.

The courier leaves without his cup lid. The client leaves me his card on the way into the meeting room, face down, like a tip.

It was a whole thing. I drink the latte anyway. It's really good, and I'm not going to the coffee place on the corner, and I keep the cup on my desk all morning, turned so the logo of the corner place faces down the room, toward $player.name's desk.

<<EndPov>>
